s the target model\u2019s probability distribution for the first draft token, the second draft token conditioned on the first, and so on.\n\nThe key trick is verification. The target model accepts draft tokens only when they are consistent with its own distribution. For each position, it compares the draft token\u2019s probability under the target model with the probability that would have been assigned if the target model had generated that token directly. If the target model would have chosen the token with sufficient likelihood, it is accepted; otherwise, the first rejected token is replaced by a token sampled from a corrected distribution that favors the target model\u2019s preferences. This rejection-sampling step is what preserves the output distribution: every token ultimately returned is sampled from the same conditional distribution the target model would have produced in ordinary autoregressive decoding, so the final text is statistically identical, not merely similar.\n\nIn practice, speedup depends on how many draft tokens are accepted before a rejection, the relative cost of the draft and target models, and the efficiency of the target model\u2019s parallel verification pass. A good draft model should be fast and aligned with the target model\u2019s behavior, while the draft length should be long enough to amortize the target pass but short enough to avoid wasting computation on rejected tokens.\n\nThe two most common reasons the speedup is smaller than hoped are low draft acceptance rates, often because the draft model is too small, poorly aligned, or the task is difficult, and", "top2": [[[1596, -0.008125565014779568], [1421, -5.577061176300049]], [[1144, -1.0609683158691041e-05], [513, -11.659274101257324]], [[4087, -0.24760742485523224], [310, -1.5658999681472778]], [[1156, -0.00131055130623281], [303, -6.819693088531494]], [[579, -0.20541076362133026], [25, -2.0897090435028076]], [[1622, -0.000630279304459691], [3296, -8.148354530334473]], [[25, -0